WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.040
Run up a flight of stairs.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.320
Your heart pounds faster.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.200
Then, over the next few minutes, it slows again.

4
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.000
On a hot, dry afternoon, tiny pores on the underside of many leaves narrow.

5
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.360
The world outside keeps changing.

6
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.360
How do living things keep their insides steady?

7
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:29.333
Biologists call this homeostasis.

8
00:00:30.253 --> 00:00:35.773
Homeostasis means keeping internal conditions within a stable range, while the

9
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:39.373
outside changes. The word range matters.

10
00:00:40.253 --> 00:00:45.753
Your body temperature is usually near 37 °C, but it shifts a little

11
00:00:45.753 --> 00:00:48.793
through the day. It is not fixed at one number.

12
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.313
Most homeostasis works by negative feedback.

13
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:58.673
In negative feedback, the response opposes the change that started it.

14
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.013
Negative does not mean bad.

15
00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:04.113
It means the response pushes the other way.

16
00:01:04.873 --> 00:01:08.713
So when a variable drifts up, the response pushes it down.

17
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.887
Every loop has the same parts.

18
00:01:12.847 --> 00:01:16.987
First, a change pushes a variable away from its usual range.

19
00:01:18.007 --> 00:01:21.627
A sensor is a cell or organ that detects the change.

20
00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:26.567
The control center compares it with the usual range, and sends a signal.

21
00:01:27.327 --> 00:01:29.407
In your body, this is usually the brain.

22
00:01:30.307 --> 00:01:35.127
An effector is a muscle, gland, or cell that carries out the response.

23
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.360
Here is the loop on a hot day.

24
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.980
Your body warms. Temperature sensors in the skin and brain detect it.

25
00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.360
The control center in the brain sends a signal.

26
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:50.980
Sweat glands release sweat.

27
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.220
The sweat evaporates, and the skin cools.

28
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.200
That response pushes the change back.

29
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.073
Plants respond too.

30
00:02:01.913 --> 00:02:04.933
A leaf has tiny pores called stomata.

31
00:02:05.793 --> 00:02:08.533
Each pore sits between two guard cells.

32
00:02:09.453 --> 00:02:13.153
The guard cells change shape to open or narrow it.

33
00:02:14.113 --> 00:02:16.673
Dry air makes a leaf lose water faster.

34
00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:22.413
In drier air, guard cells can close the pores, which slows water loss.

35
00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:26.273
So how do living things stay steady inside?

36
00:02:26.973 --> 00:02:28.293
They sense a change.

37
00:02:28.293 --> 00:02:31.633
Then they respond in a way that pushes it back.
